---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { NUCLIDES, nuclidePage, slugOf } from "../lib/nuclides";
import { dominantProgeny } from "../lib/decay-chain";
import { u } from "../lib/site";

/** ★★ 숫자를 글자로 적지 않는다 — 표도 문장도 엔진과 자료에서 파생시킨다. */
type Row = { key: string; e: number; acr: number; al: number; wat: number;
             low: number; pb: number; g: number };
const all: Row[] = Object.keys(NUCLIDES)
  .map((key) => {
    const p = nuclidePage(key), n = NUCLIDES[key];
    const of = (m: string) => p.betaRanges.find((r) => r.material === m)?.cm ?? NaN;
    return { key, e: n.beta_max_keV ?? 0, acr: of("acrylic"), al: of("aluminum"),
             wat: of("water"), low: p.bremsLow, pb: p.bremsLead, g: n.gamma_const };
  })
  .filter((r) => r.e > 0)
  .sort((a, b) => b.e - a.e);
const top = all.slice(0, 10);

/** ★★ **납/아크릴 비를 핵종별 사실로 쓰지 않는다.** 수율식이 Z 에 정비례하므로 그 비는
 *  147장 전부에서 **같은 수**다(실측: 최소와 최대가 소수점까지 같다). 쪽마다 다른 척하는
 *  숫자를 찍는 것이 곧 「틀에 값만 갈아 끼운 글」이다 — 한 번만, 식의 성질로 적는다. */
const ZR = all[0].pb / all[0].low;
const sameRatio = Math.abs(Math.max(...all.map((r) => r.pb / r.low)) -
                           Math.min(...all.map((r) => r.pb / r.low))) < 1e-9;

const mm = (cm: number, d = 3) => (Number.isFinite(cm) ? (cm * 10).toPrecision(d) : "—");
const pct = (x: number, d = 2) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(d)}%`;
const hardest = top[0];
/** 딸이 더 센 경우 — 이 쪽의 수치를 모핵종으로 고르면 안 되는 자리다. */
const withProgeny = all.filter((r) => dominantProgeny(r.key)?.betaHotter);
/** ★★ **베타 표에 감마 선원이 섞인다.** Co-60 은 종점 1.49 MeV 로 상위에 들지만 실제
 *  선원에서는 **광자장이 압도**한다 — 아무 말 없이 두면 「이 핵종은 아크릴로 막는다」로
 *  읽힌다. 문턱은 `decay-chain.ts` 가 쓰는 것과 같은 0.01 mGy·m²/(GBq·h) 다.
 *  ★ 표에서 빼지 않는다 — 종점은 맞는 값이고, 빼면 다른 거짓이 된다. **밝힌다.** */
const GAMMA_FLOOR = 0.01;
const gammaLed = top.filter((r) => r.g >= GAMMA_FLOOR).sort((a, b) => b.g - a.g);
---
<Base title="Shield materials for beta emitters"
      description="A beta shield is two decisions: how thick, and what of. The thickness follows from the endpoint energy. The material follows from bremsstrahlung, which rises with atomic number — which is why lead is the wrong thing to put first."
      schema="TechArticle">
  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">
    Shield materials for beta emitters
  </h1>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <p>
      Stopping a beta is two separate decisions, and only the first one is about thickness. A few
      millimetres of almost anything will absorb the electrons. What the electrons leave behind on
      the way is decided by what you chose to stop them with, and that is where lead — the reflex
      answer for every other kind of radiation — is the wrong material to reach for first.
    </p>

    {/* ★ 이 판의 라벨 넉 장은 **생성 모델이 그린 것**이다(2026-09-24, 규칙을 실측으로 고쳤다).
        원본 해상도 2배 확대로 읽어 확인했다 — ACRYLIC · BETA · LEAD · X-RAYS 전부 정확하다.
        ★★ 다만 **자료에서 나온 숫자는 여전히 그리지 않는다** — 그림 속 숫자는 자료가 바뀌어도
          따라오지 않아 조용히 낡는다. 수치는 `figcaption` 과 본문이 든다. */}
    <figure class="mt-8 plate not-prose">
      <img src={u("/img/plates/beta-shield-materials.webp")}
           srcset={`${u("/img/plates/beta-shield-materials-768.webp")} 768w, ${u("/img/plates/beta-shield-materials.webp")} 1536w`}
           sizes="(min-width: 768px) 768px, 100vw"
           width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Isometric pencil drawing of a beta shielding stack. A plain unmarked source capsule stands at the left of a machined base. Two panels stand upright further along: first a thick clear acrylic panel, then after a gap a much thinner dark cross-hatched lead plate. Dotted electron tracks labelled BETA leave the capsule and all come to rest inside the acrylic. From those stopping points wavy lines labelled X-RAYS continue through the rest of the acrylic, cross the gap and end inside the lead plate. Leader lines label the two panels ACRYLIC and LEAD." />
      <figcaption>
        The order is the whole point. The <b>acrylic</b> stops the electrons; the X-rays it makes in
        doing so are then absorbed by a much thinner sheet of <b>lead</b> behind it. Put the lead
        first and it becomes the X-ray source instead. At the hardest endpoint carried here
        ({(hardest.e / 1000).toFixed(2)} MeV) the converted fraction is {pct(hardest.low)} in
        acrylic against {pct(hardest.pb)} in lead.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Thickness follows from the endpoint</h2>
    <p>
      The range of a beta is set by the most energetic electrons in the spectrum, not the average
      ones, so a shield is sized on the endpoint. {hardest.key} has the hardest beta in this
      dataset at {(hardest.e / 1000).toFixed(2)} MeV, and it is stopped by {mm(hardest.acr)} mm of
      acrylic or {mm(hardest.al)} mm of aluminium. Nothing in this dataset needs more than that.
    </p>
    <div class="pin-first mt-3">
    <table class="lg:min-w-[40rem]">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Nuclide</th><th>Endpoint (MeV)</th><th>Acrylic (mm)</th>
            <th class="hidden lg:table-cell">Aluminium (mm)</th>
            <th class="hidden lg:table-cell">Water (mm)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {top.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={u(`/nuclides/${slugOf(r.key)}/`)} class="underline">{r.key}</a></td>
            <td>{(r.e / 1000).toFixed(2)}</td>
            <td>{mm(r.acr)}</td>
            <td class="hidden lg:table-cell">{mm(r.al)}</td>
            <td class="hidden lg:table-cell">{mm(r.wat)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px]">
      The ten hardest beta emitters carried here, of {all.length}. Ranges are Katz–Penfold fits to
      the endpoint: stopping thicknesses rather than attenuation lengths, good to about ±10%.
    </p>
    {gammaLed.length > 0 && (
      <p>
        Being on that list does not make a nuclide a beta shielding problem.
        {" "}{gammaLed.map((r) => r.key).join(", ")} {gammaLed.length === 1 ? "is" : "are"} carried
        here for {gammaLed.length === 1 ? "its" : "their"} endpoint, but in a real source the photon
        field dominates — {gammaLed[0].key} has an air kerma rate constant of
        {" "}{gammaLed[0].g.toPrecision(3)} mGy·m²/(GBq·h), and a few millimetres of acrylic does
        nothing about that. For {gammaLed.length === 1 ? "it" : "those"} the beta shield is a
        secondary detail inside a photon shield, not the shield.
      </p>
    )}

    <h2>Material follows from bremsstrahlung</h2>
    <p>
      An electron brought to rest radiates part of its energy as X-rays. The fraction rises with the
      atomic number of the absorber, and it rises with the energy of the electron. Stop a hard beta
      in lead and a shield that was supposed to remove a short-range, easily absorbed radiation has
      manufactured a penetrating one in its place, inside the shield, where it is hardest to deal
      with. Stop the same beta in acrylic and the same conversion happens, but far less of it.
    </p>
    <div class="pin-first mt-3">
    <table class="lg:min-w-[34rem]">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Nuclide</th><th>Endpoint (MeV)</th>
            <th>Converted in acrylic</th><th>Converted in lead</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {top.map((r) => (
          <tr><td>{r.key}</td><td>{(r.e / 1000).toFixed(2)}</td>
              <td>{pct(r.low)}</td><td>{pct(r.pb)}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px]">
      {sameRatio && (
        <>The ratio between the two columns is {ZR.toFixed(1)} on every row, and it is the same on
        every beta emitter in this dataset. That is not a measurement: the yield used here is
        proportional to atomic number, so the ratio is fixed at 82/6 by the formula itself. What
        actually varies from nuclide to nuclide is the size of the fractions, which follows the
        endpoint — from {pct(Math.min(...all.map((r) => r.low)), 3)} to {pct(Math.max(...all.map((r) => r.low)))}
        {" "}in acrylic across the {all.length} beta emitters here.</>
      )}
    </p>

    <h2>Low atomic number first, high atomic number behind</h2>
    <p>
      The practical arrangement follows from those two facts. The electrons meet a low-Z absorber
      first — acrylic, aluminium, or water in a tank — chosen thick enough to exceed the range from
      the first table. Whatever X-rays that layer does produce then meet a second, thin, high-Z
      layer placed behind it, where lead is exactly the right material, because photons are what it
      is good at. Reverse the order and the first layer becomes the X-ray source.
    </p>
    <p>
      Whether the second layer is needed at all is an activity question rather than a material one.
      The converted fraction is small: at the hardest endpoint here it is {pct(hardest.low)} in
      acrylic. At kilobecquerel activities that is nothing. At the tens of gigabecquerels used in
      industrial sources it is not.
    </p>

    {withProgeny.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2>Which endpoint to size on</h2>
        <p>
          For {withProgeny.length} of the nuclides here the endpoint in the first table is not the
          one that decides either answer. Each of them sits above a short-lived daughter with a
          harder beta, and a source contains both, so both the thickness and the converted fraction
          have to be taken from the daughter.
          {" "}<a href={u("/daughter-shielding/")} class="underline">Nuclides whose daughter sets the
          shield</a> lists them with the factor by which the parent figure understates the
          thickness.
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <h2>Limits of these figures</h2>
    <p>
      The converted fractions here come from a thick-target rule of thumb, linear in atomic number
      and in endpoint energy. It is an order-of-magnitude guide whose purpose is to show why the
      material matters, and it is not a substitute for a transport calculation: it says nothing
      about where inside the absorber the X-rays are made, what their spectrum is, or how much of
      it escapes. The ranges carry their own ±10%. Neither figure accounts for the container the
      source already sits in, which for a sealed source may be doing most of the work described
      here before anything is added.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li><a href={u("/beta/")} class="underline">Beta dose rate and shielding</a> — range,
        transmission and converted fraction for an endpoint and absorber you choose.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/daughter-shielding/")} class="underline">Nuclides whose daughter sets the
        shield</a> — the cases where the endpoint above is the wrong one.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/methods/")} class="underline">Methods and data</a> — the formulas and where
        the decay data come from.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/dataset-limits/")} class="underline">Limits of this dataset</a> — what these
        approximations do and do not cover.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
